# Ordered source patch series applied to the pinned upstream Pi tree, one filename per line.
#
# Order is this file's order and nothing else. Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored; a patch file
# in this directory that no line names is drift and fails preparation. Names must look like
# `NNNN-short-description.patch`.
#
# Pinned to Pi 1.4.0. These are the four seams the shipped native dashboard uses, rebased from the 0.84.3
# series and regenerated against this order: 0002 (ingress, return and terminal-response authorization),
# 0003 (presentation and readiness over the bootstrap sideband), 0005 (child terminal handoff) and 0007
# (input-listener provenance). They keep their 0.84.3 numbers so a reader can follow each one back.
#
# 0001 (submission barrier), 0004 (canonical admission) and 0006 (bootstrap protocol 2, published input target
# and presentation markers) are not carried. Nothing shipped activated them: 0001 and 0004 act only once an
# extension calls `setNativeSubmissionHost`, which neither shipped extension does, and 0006's additions are
# opt-in through `PI_NATIVE_BOOTSTRAP_PROTOCOL=2`, which the manager never sets, or are used only by
# `runtime/pty-host.mjs`, which the native dashboard does not load. They remain in git history.
#
# Conflicts resolved against 1.4.0: 0002's terminal-reply authorization keeps upstream's device-attributes
# accounting and OSC 7501 program-status handling (program-status replies are not host-authorized), and its
# origin ledger follows upstream's new Alt-prefix split in StdinBuffer. 0003's frame receipt counts the bytes
# of 1.4.0's chunked main-screen writer. 0005 and 0007 needed only import and anchor updates.
0002-native-ingress-return-profile.patch
0003-native-presentation-readiness.patch
0005-native-child-handoff.patch
0007-native-input-listener-provenance.patch
